WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Epidemiology questions in the HSC tend to ask

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:04.360
for three things.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.360
Calculate a rate from new data, draw a

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.000
graph of rates, and judge whether a study

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.200
supports its claim.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.040
Here are three HSC-style questions, one of each.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.320
Pause and try each one properly, then we'll

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.520
work through it together.

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:21.613
Question one.

00:00:22.174 --> 00:00:25.133
Two regions recorded type 2 diabetes for one

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:25.853
year.

00:00:26.174 --> 00:00:29.213
Region P has one point two million people,

00:00:29.294 --> 00:00:32.333
with six thousand six hundred new diagnoses and

00:00:32.414 --> 00:00:33.893
three hundred deaths.

00:00:34.254 --> 00:00:36.813
Region Q has a hundred and fifty thousand

00:00:36.894 --> 00:00:40.013
people, with one thousand and fifty new diagnoses

00:00:40.254 --> 00:00:41.613
and fifty-four deaths.

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:45.213
Calculate the incidence and mortality rates per hundred

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:46.733
thousand for each region.

00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:50.253
Then evaluate the claim that Region P has

00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:52.173
the worse diabetes problem.

00:00:52.494 --> 00:00:53.853
Pause here to answer.

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:08.986
Rates first, laid out as equation, substitute, solve,

00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:10.026
sense check.

00:01:10.347 --> 00:01:13.866
Incidence is new cases over the population, times

00:01:13.947 --> 00:01:15.306
a hundred thousand.

00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:18.266
For P, six thousand six hundred over one

00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:21.226
point two million, times a hundred thousand, is

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:23.786
five hundred and fifty per hundred thousand per

00:01:23.947 --> 00:01:24.826
year.

00:01:24.907 --> 00:01:27.386
For Q, one thousand and fifty over a

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:30.346
hundred and fifty thousand gives seven hundred.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.826
Mortality puts deaths on top.

00:01:32.947 --> 00:01:35.386
Three hundred over one point two million gives

00:01:35.507 --> 00:01:38.506
twenty-five, and fifty-four over a hundred and fifty

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:40.546
thousand gives thirty-six.

00:01:40.907 --> 00:01:44.106
Sense check: Q is the smaller region, yet

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:45.786
both its rates are higher.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:47.800
Now the claim.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.080
P had more cases mainly because it has

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.920
eight times as many people.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.120
Out of every hundred thousand people, Q had

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.920
more new cases and more deaths, so the

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.560
claim isn't supported.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.840
But before saying people in Q face a

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.080
higher risk, check their ages.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.600
Type 2 diabetes becomes more common with age,

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:10.920
so an older population would have a higher

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.160
rate, even if the risk at each age

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:13.880
were the same.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.800
Age-standardised rates, adjusted as if both regions had

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.440
the same age mix, would make that comparison

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.160
fairer.

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:25.406
The classic mistake is comparing raw counts.

00:02:25.647 --> 00:02:28.606
A bigger population gives more cases even when

00:02:28.687 --> 00:02:30.086
the risk is the same.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.926
Here, the region with fewer cases had the

00:02:33.007 --> 00:02:34.166
higher rates.

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:36.926
Turn every count into a rate before you

00:02:37.007 --> 00:02:37.726
compare.

00:02:38.934 --> 00:02:39.813
Question two.

00:02:40.374 --> 00:02:43.493
The table gives melanoma incidence and mortality rates

00:02:43.574 --> 00:02:46.773
in Australia, per hundred thousand people, from 1985

00:02:46.854 --> 00:02:49.173
to 2020.

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.333
Draw a line graph of both rates on

00:02:51.414 --> 00:02:53.013
one set of axes.

00:02:53.094 --> 00:02:55.813
Then describe the trend in each rate, and

00:02:56.094 --> 00:02:58.933
explain why the two rates differ so much.

00:02:59.094 --> 00:03:00.373
Pause here to answer.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.520
Set up the graph before you plot anything.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.080
Year goes on the x axis, because it's

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.440
the independent variable.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.920
The rate goes on the y axis, with

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.880
its unit, per hundred thousand people per year.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.920
Both rates share that unit, so one axis

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:26.400
works.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.360
Look at the years: they jump by ten,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.680
then by five at the end, so space

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.640
them to scale.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.360
For the y axis, choose an even scale

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.800
that fits the largest value, fifty-two: zero to

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.320
sixty in tens.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.040
Plot each point, then join each set with

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.520
its own ruled line, because each value is

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.160
a whole year's rate, and add a key

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.280
and a title.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.240
That's TAILS: title, axes, intervals, labels, scale.

00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:57.666
Now describe and explain.

00:03:58.027 --> 00:04:02.426
Incidence rose from thirty-eight in 1985 to fifty-two

00:04:02.587 --> 00:04:05.786
in 2015, then fell slightly to fifty.

00:04:06.427 --> 00:04:09.866
Mortality stayed far lower, around six, and fell

00:04:09.947 --> 00:04:12.506
to about five by 2020.

00:04:12.587 --> 00:04:15.546
They differ because most melanomas are found early,

00:04:15.627 --> 00:04:18.106
while they're still thin, and cut out, so

00:04:18.187 --> 00:04:21.226
far fewer people die than are diagnosed.

00:04:21.387 --> 00:04:23.866
Deaths in one year include people diagnosed in

00:04:23.947 --> 00:04:26.906
earlier years, so these lines don't directly give

00:04:26.987 --> 00:04:28.106
survival.

00:04:28.147 --> 00:04:30.906
The recent fall in deaths fits better treatment,

00:04:30.987 --> 00:04:33.066
but this graph alone can't show that.

00:04:34.267 --> 00:04:36.826
The classic mistake is starting the y axis

00:04:36.947 --> 00:04:39.066
at thirty, to fit the incidence line.

00:04:39.547 --> 00:04:42.146
Then the mortality points fall off the graph.

00:04:42.427 --> 00:04:45.146
Choose one scale that fits every value.

00:04:45.467 --> 00:04:47.546
And space the years to scale, or the

00:04:47.627 --> 00:04:49.546
last five years look like ten.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:51.646
Question three.

00:04:51.727 --> 00:04:54.926
A survey asked two thousand adults in one

00:04:55.007 --> 00:04:57.486
city whether they drink an energy drink every

00:04:57.567 --> 00:05:00.686
day, and whether they're currently living with diagnosed

00:05:00.847 --> 00:05:02.206
type 2 diabetes.

00:05:02.847 --> 00:05:06.766
Of four hundred daily drinkers, thirty-six had diabetes.

00:05:07.327 --> 00:05:11.246
Of sixteen hundred other adults, sixty-four had diabetes.

00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:14.686
A website claims that energy drinks cause type

00:05:14.767 --> 00:05:15.726
2 diabetes.

00:05:16.287 --> 00:05:19.246
Calculate the prevalence of diabetes in each group.

00:05:19.647 --> 00:05:22.126
Then evaluate the method and the claim.

00:05:22.687 --> 00:05:23.966
Pause here to answer.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.360
Prevalence is existing cases out of the group,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.400
as a percentage.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.480
Thirty-six over four hundred is nine per cent.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:45.920
Sixty-four over sixteen hundred is four per cent,

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.560
so diabetes was about twice as common among

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:49.920
daily drinkers.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.440
Now name the design.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.400
Both things were measured at one moment, so

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.040
it's a cross-sectional study, a snapshot.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.800
Its strength: it's quick, and it measures prevalence

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.160
among the respondents.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:04.886
Now the limitations.

00:06:05.127 --> 00:06:08.246
A snapshot can't show which came first, so

00:06:08.407 --> 00:06:10.726
some people may have changed their drinking after

00:06:10.807 --> 00:06:11.686
diagnosis.

00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:15.446
Daily drinkers may also differ in diet, weight,

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:18.006
sleep or age, and any of those could

00:06:18.047 --> 00:06:21.046
be the real cause, so they're possible confounders.

00:06:21.847 --> 00:06:25.846
The answers were self-reported, online, from one city.

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:28.526
So the data show an association, not a

00:06:28.567 --> 00:06:31.686
cause, and the claim goes too far.

00:06:31.767 --> 00:06:34.646
A stronger test is a cohort: record drinks

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.846
in adults without diabetes, follow them forward, and

00:06:37.927 --> 00:06:41.046
compare who develops it, adjusting for confounders.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:45.146
The classic mistake is thinking two thousand people

00:06:45.227 --> 00:06:46.506
makes the claim proven.

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:50.586
More responses can reduce random uncertainty, but don't

00:06:50.747 --> 00:06:53.506
remove selection bias or fix the design.

00:06:53.787 --> 00:06:56.186
A snapshot still can't show a cause.

00:06:56.587 --> 00:06:58.906
For that, you need the order in time,

00:06:59.027 --> 00:07:00.986
and a way to deal with confounders.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.120
So, here's your first move for each type.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.360
For a rate, divide by the right population,

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:11.160
and use the same unit for every group.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.040
For a graph, set up the axes, units

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.000
and an even scale that fits every value,

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.520
before you plot.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.400
For a study, name the design, then judge

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.640
what it can and can't show.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.480
Next: how type 2 diabetes can be managed.

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.140
Here are the key notes.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.300
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.180
to copy them.
